From 731b1dd208320964608a992508182e8908c3cf5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: desktop-atlas <desktop@atlas.local>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 13:42:39 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Windows port: suite 100% green (1024 passed, 0 failed, was 44
 failures)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The dourmouse suite was written and proven on macOS; on Windows 44 tests
failed. All were portability issues, not engine bugs — three classes:

1. Fake-CLI fixtures were POSIX bash scripts Windows can't execute
   (WinError 193). Claude/codex fakes are now .cmd shims on Windows; the
   atlas fake is a real interpreter copy + a real atlas.ops.cli module in
   the fake repo (the exact subprocess path production takes), with
   PYTHONHOME silencing the copied interpreter's prefix warning.
2. Files read with the process-default cp1252 codec mangled UTF-8 content
   (em-dashes became mojibake). All text reads now pass encoding="utf-8"
   explicitly, in both production and test helpers. Also found a real
   production bug this exposed: os.sysconf doesn't exist on Windows — the
   system-info memory line now uses GlobalMemoryStatusEx via ctypes.
3. POSIX path semantics don't hold on Windows. Repo-index facts are now
   POSIX-normalized (shared knowledge must look identical everywhere);
   the system-access guard gained Windows system roots (C:\Windows,
   Program Files, ProgramData) matched case-insensitively — a real
   security gap, not just a test fix; path-guard tests are now
   platform-aware; a "terminal command" query routes deterministically to
   the system agent via the domain table (was tie-breaking to atlas_cmd).

Also fixed: the upload handler now drains the request body before
rejecting a bad filename — Windows closes a socket holding unread
received data via RST, so clients saw ConnectionAbortedError (WinError
10053) instead of the 400 response.
